# UAD Report Pricing

## Recommendation

A retail price of **$2.50 per report** is appropriate, provided the product produces a submission-ready report and includes meaningful validation, understandable error reporting, and regeneration after corrections.

The recommended price positioning is:

| Customer type | Suggested price per report |
|---|---:|
| Retail or pay-as-you-go | $2.50 |
| Normal-volume customer | $1.50–$2.00 effective price |
| Large AMC, lender, or software integration | $0.75–$1.50 |

The $2.50 retail price should serve as the public price anchor. Volume packages and negotiated enterprise pricing should result in a lower realized price for most recurring customers.

## Definition of a Billable Report

One billable report should correspond to one appraisal engagement or DocFile ID. The price should include:

- unlimited previews;
- corrections made during the appraisal workflow;
- regenerated versions of the report; and
- validation performed before final submission.

Customers should not be charged for every revision of the same report. Charging repeatedly while an appraiser corrects or completes a report would feel punitive and could discourage use of the validation workflow.

## Value Supporting the Price

Whether $2.50 is appropriate depends on what the product does.

If the product only renders already-valid XML as a PDF, $2.50 may be high. If the product validates UAD 3.6 data, explains failures, generates the report, preserves traceability, and reduces rejected submissions, $2.50 is inexpensive relative to the labor saved and the value of the appraisal transaction.

The retail offer should therefore emphasize outcomes rather than file conversion:

- submission readiness;
- earlier identification of compliance problems;
- understandable validation findings;
- fewer rejected submissions and avoidable revisions;
- repeatable generation of the report; and
- traceability between source data, validation results, and the generated artifact.

## Suggested Launch Structure

During the voluntary adoption period, introductory pricing can reduce the barrier to evaluation and help users incorporate UAD 3.6 into their workflows before the mandate.

| Offering                 | Suggested price |
|--------------------------|----------------:|
| Evaluation reports       |            Free |
| Individual retail report |           $2.50 |
| 50-report package        |             $99 |
| 250-report package       |            $375 |
| High-volume or API usage |      Negotiated |

This structure preserves the $2.50 retail anchor while rewarding commitment and volume.

## Payment Processing

Individual $2.50 card transactions should be avoided because transaction fees would consume an unnecessarily large portion of the price. Prefer:

- prepaid report credits;
- report bundles;
- a stored customer balance; or
- monthly invoicing based on completed reports.

## UAD 3.6 Transition Date

The official GSE mandate begins **November 2, 2026**. Through November 1, 2026, lenders may submit either UAD 2.6 or UAD 3.6 appraisal reports. Beginning November 2, all new appraisal reports submitted to UCDP must use UAD 3.6. Revisions to qualifying UAD 2.6 reports submitted before the mandate may continue during the transition period.

Official references:

- [Fannie Mae: UCDP Submission Summary Report Messaging Updates Supporting the Transition to UAD 3.6](https://singlefamily.fanniemae.com/news-events/ucdp-submission-summary-report-messaging-updates-supporting-transition-uad-36)
- [Freddie Mac: Uniform Appraisal Dataset 3.6 FAQ](https://sf.freddiemac.com/faqs/uad-and-forms-redesign)

## Pricing Decision

Launch with **$2.50 as the retail price per report**, design bundles around a **$1.50–$2.00 realized price**, and offer negotiated volume pricing to large AMCs, lenders, and integrated software providers. Treat $2.50 as the retail anchor rather than the price every customer must ultimately pay.
